+# DirectiveAttack - Directive Fleet doctrine, forked from MultiAttack 2026-09-02.
+#
+# Based on AI/Compound/MultiAttack.py from Star Trek: Bridge Commander,
+# (c) Totally Games / Activision.  This header and six changed values are new;
+# everything else is Totally Games' code and remains theirs.
+#
+# Stock MultiAttack has three faults, all observed live in a DS9Wings mirror:
+#
+#   1. It stops closing at 500 (ConditionInRange + SetInterceptDistance) while
+#      DS9Wings' 36 beams reach only 250.  It parks at double its own main
+#      battery's range and fights with a quarter of its guns.
+#   2. Its whole movement chain sits in a SequenceAI with SetResetIfInterrupted(1),
+#      so incoming fire throws it back to child one - the gunnery block - and it
+#      never reaches the movement slice at all.  Under sustained fire it stops
+#      manoeuvring entirely.
+#   3. Gunnery and movement are SERIAL children of one sequence on a 4s/1s split,
+#      so it can never shoot and move at once and only manoeuvres 20% of the time.
+#      Whether two such ships fight evenly is a coin-flip on cycle phase, and the
+#      loser of that coin-flip is locked out permanently: it stops firing, its
+#      shields collapse, and the resulting interrupts keep it reset forever.
+#
+# Fixes here: close to 220 (inside the beams), never reset on interrupt, and an
+# even 2s/2s slice.  Structural fix - firing from a PreprocessingAI wrapped round
+# the movement, the way FedAttack does it - is deliberately NOT attempted; that is
+# a rewrite, not a tune.  Stock MultiAttack is left alone for its other 21 hulls.
+
